WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.660
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Charles Wheelan, who is professor of public

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:10.380
policy at Dartmouth, author of a number of
books, many of which I've read, including

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.430
a Naked Economics, Naked Money, Naked Statistics.
Great to have you on today.

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:19.680
Great. I'm glad you've read all the naked
books that we reported to this series.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.840
So you taught well in the book. You talk a
lot, particularly in naked economics and naked

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:32.399
money, about the impact and repercussions
at a macroeconomic level of the government

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:37.960
doing things which include making adjustments
to the money supply, making money available

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:43.059
in different ways. So maybe to pick something
that's going on now when you look at the so-called

00:00:43.059 --> 00:00:48.420
coronavirus stimulus and how that was administered
partially to individuals, partially as loans,

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:53.730
partially to corporations, what about it from
a broad economic standpoint was done correctly

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:58.469
and what was not so good?
I think the best thing is that it put money

00:00:58.469 --> 00:01:03.499
in the pockets of people who needed it, small
businesses. And we'll get to how well it was

00:01:03.499 --> 00:01:08.060
targeted because that anticipates, I think,
one of the weaknesses and also consumers who

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:13.140
are at risk of missing rent payments, being
evicted and the like. One of the most intriguing

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:20.219
concepts in economics is the paradox of thrift.
This idea that when something bad happens

00:01:20.219 --> 00:01:25.880
are natural and entirely rational. Response
is to spend less, to hang on to what we have.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.979
And of course, as soon as we hang on to what
we have, then we don't buy our refrigerator,

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:34.021
don't buy a car, don't go out to dinner. And
that's the income of all of those folks. They

00:01:34.021 --> 00:01:39.609
hang on more tightly. And of course, more
businesses are in distress to somehow government

00:01:39.609 --> 00:01:45.570
needs to overcome that paradox of thrift,
not in the financial crisis. The problem was

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:50.469
that all our financial institutions were paralyzed.
Nobody could get credit. If you want to go

00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:54.409
to a restaurant, the restaurant was open.
But, you know, how can we open the spigots

00:01:54.409 --> 00:01:59.759
of credit again? So the economy worked with
covid. We've got an entirely different problem.

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:05.420
The banking system is mercifully healthy and
many people went into covid in pretty good

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:09.450
financial shape, but they couldn't spend the
money they had because the restaurant was

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:16.010
closed. So the challenge there was to keep
those restaurants afloat until we could get

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:20.280
through. And I guess we're now seeing the
end of the tunnel, the pandemic, just keep

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:24.300
them afloat as opposed to starting up, say,
the financial system again.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:28.890
Yeah, it seems when I look at the way some
European countries and other countries handled

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:34.590
this, they were more aggressive with the idea
of we will pay the salaries of your employees

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:39.000
so that you don't lay anybody off. I think
the idea being that there would be less friction

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.980
in getting things back up to speed because
the the employees would still be employees

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:47.910
and so on. Do you think that that was not
done well enough in the US?

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:52.150
I think our approach was probably a little
more scattershot. Some of the payments to

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:57.730
businesses had the effect of keeping employees
on the books. On the other end, I talked to

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:03.840
small business people who faced a dilemma
whereby if they were kept on at reduced salary,

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.670
their employees might have done better if
they went on unemployment. Right. And so you

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:11.800
created some kind of tension there. And then
obviously what we've been reading in the news

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:16.460
in the last couple of weeks, about a high
proportion of these payments going to a small

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:21.480
number of firms. I haven't delved into that.
But that does get to a question you always

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:27.580
have to face, which is how well targeted is
this to the folks who really need support

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:32.470
in the near term? That's priority number one.
Priority number two is how well targeted is

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:37.670
it to the folks who will continue to spend
in ways that keep other businesses healthy?

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:43.070
And what what are the answers to those three
questions that so many of you know, it strikes

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:48.190
me as given the time allotted, that it was
probably about a B plus. OK, you know, we

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:53.010
forget that nobody was. Well, it's not true.
Nobody was planning for this, but it certainly

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:58.290
wasn't what we expected to come along. And
when you've got to draw up something like

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:03.540
this so quickly and deal with the politics,
get it through a very divided Congress, I

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:08.110
think we did actually reasonably well up to
the present. We're now, again, we're arguing

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:12.610
over what the next phase of it ought to be.
Yeah, I mean, I think that we can talk about

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:18.300
the lack of pandemic response readiness by
the administration on the more medical side,

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.450
getting rid of the pandemic response team,
ignoring the playbook, et cetera. But the

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:26.750
shock to the system from the economic side
would have been a shock. Regardless, it sounds

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:29.370
like, is what you're saying.
I think that's true. And I think there's some

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:34.970
things that seem superficially attractive
that have some downsides. So, for example,

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:39.430
the moratorium on evictions and moratorium,
I think it makes perfect sense that you do

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:42.620
not want to be throwing people out on the
street in the middle of a pandemic. On the

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:46.820
other hand, you do have to appreciate that
those folks who are not paying rent are not

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:51.320
paying rent to other small property owners
who will then have trouble. So, you know,

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:56.500
I'm much more comfortable with something that
substitutes for those rent. Payments, right?

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:00.990
There's no eviction and therefore keeps the
property owners whole as opposed to just throwing

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:05.870
the thrust of the pain on these small renters,
many of whom have just a couple of apartments

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:09.010
that are a source of revenue. And if we say
you can't evict them, but they're not paying

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:13.370
your rent, then you've just created a different
level of distress.

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:20.630
There is a perspective in economic thinking.
Any time you're talking about giving people

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:26.621
money, this applies to stimulus. This applies
to programs like universal basic income. This

00:05:26.621 --> 00:05:32.420
applies to minimum wage increases. And the
very general idea is you devalue the currency

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:38.040
when you when you make it easier to get money,
when you increase the supply of money, simple

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:43.560
supply and demand suggests each dollar becomes
worth less. There is inflation and you end

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:50.090
up basically in the exact same place. Can
you talk now about the additional knowledge

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:55.389
we have, including from behavioral economics
and other types of economic thinking that

00:05:55.389 --> 00:06:01.530
that makes that less cut and dry?
Well, the one great puzzle of our time, Prie

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:07.230
covid, is why we're not seeing more inflation.
We have pumped going back to the financial

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:12.520
crisis, up to and including the covid response,
we have pumped just staggering amounts of

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:19.540
money and liquidity into the pipes and we're
barely reaching our inflation target targets

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:25.460
and sometimes failing to reach our inflation
target. So if you went back to grad school

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:28.830
when I was in grad school in the early 90s
at the University of Chicago and said, hey,

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:33.440
let me give you a hypothetical experiment
where we pump trillions of dollars into the

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:38.560
economy, do you think you're going to get
price increases or maybe even a little deflation?

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:42.680
Everybody would have said that is impossible.
We're still trying to kind of work through

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:47.040
that part of the behavioral economics explanation.
And just let me say parenthetically, I think

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:51.330
behavioral econ is one of the most interesting
and exciting things to come along because

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:55.620
it helps to answer some of these questions
is that when you become conditioned to stable

00:06:55.620 --> 00:07:00.490
prices for a long period of time, then it's
harder to raise prices. It's the flip side

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:04.090
of the 70s when everyone just assumed, hey,
we're going to raise, but I don't care what's

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:07.240
going on. The macro, my prices are going up
15 percent this year just because that's what

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:12.150
you do in spring. You know, maybe we're seeing
the back side of that. There's some discussion

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:18.550
about whether globalization has just continued
to increase the global supply so much that

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:24.500
we can absorb the money and and spend it around
the world. And the competition from China

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:30.600
and Vietnam keeps prices low because American
firms can't raise prices. It just keeps that

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:35.460
constant pressure. My colleague here, Danny
Blanchflower, has argued that a lot of the

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:41.389
labor market data are very misleading. Unemployment
looks really low. But when you delve into

00:07:41.389 --> 00:07:45.800
it, a lot of folks are not working as many
hours as they would like to. They're feeling

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:50.139
more tenuously attached to the labor market.
And then he's found this interesting thing

00:07:50.139 --> 00:07:57.110
where if you ask people how likely they are
to be fired. Or lose their job, they overstate

00:07:57.110 --> 00:08:02.010
it by like five fold, you know, because you
can then follow them and find out how many

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:06.830
of them are actually fired. What that means
is that they are very reticent to ask for

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:10.370
more money because they feel that their job
is more precarious than it actually is. And

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:14.169
that is another thing, keeping prices low.
So it's probably some combination of these

00:08:14.169 --> 00:08:18.990
things. But I suspect that if you come up
with some unifying theory of why all this

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:23.090
money flowing into the system hasn't led to
more inflation, you will certainly be on the

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:27.040
Nobel short list.
Well, on the other side of this example that

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:32.289
we're seeing in, I think in both naked economics
and money, although you'll you'll correct

00:08:32.289 --> 00:08:39.669
me, you often use my birth country of Argentina
as an example of disasters when it comes to

00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:44.920
currency. I mean, we've we had, you know,
the peg, the dollar peso peg, which completely

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:51.860
failed hyperinflation, not at the level of
of like Zimbabwe or Venezuela, but but still

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:57.360
pretty bad. All these terrible situations
when it comes to currency, what seems to be

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:04.290
the tipping point where those cascading currency
disasters happen in countries?

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:09.730
I think the difference between Argentina and
most places in the world is that there's just

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:14.360
not a lot of credibility. People don't believe
the central bank. They don't believe the fiscal

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:19.439
authorities. And a lot of monetary policy
is about credible commitment that you say

00:09:19.439 --> 00:09:23.720
you're going to do something, you do it. The
markets respond in ways that if they believe

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:29.019
you reinforce what's going to happen. So when
the chair of the Fed in the United States

00:09:29.019 --> 00:09:33.540
says we're serious about inflation, we're
not going to let prices rise faster than two

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:38.110
percent ever, well, then people don't raise
their prices because they assume that the

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:43.959
Fed would fight that back. And it becomes
a self reinforcing prophecy when the monetary

00:09:43.959 --> 00:09:47.999
authorities in Argentina say, hey, we're going
to fight inflation. What everybody's done

00:09:47.999 --> 00:09:53.879
laughing. They say, yeah, we've seen this
movie 11 times before. I I mean, it's been

00:09:53.879 --> 00:09:58.560
a long time since I wrote Naked Money. We
were going to put a whole chapter on Argentina

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:02.800
and instead we just used a lot of examples.
Yeah. That I'm glad you didn't do the whole

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:07.529
chapter. That would have been that would have
been more devastating. So so when it comes

00:10:07.529 --> 00:10:12.660
to in naked money, when you talk about the
credibility of money, as you're kind of talking

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:17.170
about in the context of Argentina, you do
address cryptocurrency and you and you take

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:22.279
head on some of the bullet points that we
hear from some of the anti cryptocurrency

00:10:22.279 --> 00:10:27.100
people, such as it doesn't have inherent value.
And you explain that, you know, the idea of

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:33.850
gold having inherent value is also less clear
maybe than some people point out. What's your

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:40.170
sense now? Since the book was written about
cryptocurrency, we've seen, you know, early

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:45.320
in the pandemic, people expecting Bitcoin
to go crazy, were disappointed that it didn't.

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:49.360
But then in the last few months, we've actually
seen significant increase in the value of

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:55.779
Bitcoin. What's your sense of it now?
I'm still not very sold on the idea that Bitcoin

00:10:55.779 --> 00:11:01.330
is here for the long run. A number of reasons
to be part of. You expect money to do a number

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:06.670
of things, and I don't think Bitcoin does
any of them very well. So one is as a unit

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:11.309
of exchange and this is the easiest one where
it's clearly failed me. Part of the reason

00:11:11.309 --> 00:11:16.209
that we use dollars and not Argentine pesos
is we have a pretty good idea of what the

00:11:16.209 --> 00:11:20.899
dollar is going to be worth next year, even
ten years, maybe plus or minus two percent.

00:11:20.899 --> 00:11:25.399
But it really doesn't move a whole lot. Yeah,
with Bitcoin, right. When people say, wow,

00:11:25.399 --> 00:11:30.579
Bitcoin is on awards up four hundred percent,
that's not a good thing from the standpoint

00:11:30.579 --> 00:11:35.999
of money. Now, if you invested in it, you
made a lot of money. But the point of money

00:11:35.999 --> 00:11:40.519
is I'm going to buy a house and I'm going
to say, you know, David, I'm going to give

00:11:40.519 --> 00:11:45.399
you one hundred bitcoins a year for the next
30 years. That's the way a mortgage works.

00:11:45.399 --> 00:11:50.249
Now, if those bitcoins go up four hundred
percent in value, my mortgage payment just

00:11:50.249 --> 00:11:55.720
went up four percent. So it's very hard for
us to contract using a monetary, you know,

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:59.910
that's flexible. But even if it's going up,
you just want something stable, that the other

00:11:59.910 --> 00:12:07.199
things that we look for are is it a good store
of value? That one, I think, is also uncertain.

00:12:07.199 --> 00:12:12.730
Yes, it's gain value, but it's also plummeted
at times on that one.

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:18.720
Charles, after how many years of of at least
it retaining some value, would we be able

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:22.059
to say that the store of value requirement
is met?

00:12:22.059 --> 00:12:25.949
I don't know if it's a number of years. I
think it's the the percentage of people who

00:12:25.949 --> 00:12:30.429
are persuaded that it will hold its value.
I mean, because as you correctly pointed out,

00:12:30.429 --> 00:12:36.699
it's all whether I believe that it has value.
And if I do, then I'll use it. So certainly

00:12:36.699 --> 00:12:42.130
more than it used to. Yeah. And then the last
one is kind of as a unit of account or not

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:47.149
as kind of but as a medium of exchange. So
how easy is it to do transactions. And it's

00:12:47.149 --> 00:12:52.069
still much easier for me to use a credit card
or use cash than it is for Bitcoin. I've got

00:12:52.069 --> 00:12:56.050
to go buy the bitcoins and so that.
I guess let me push back slightly on that

00:12:56.050 --> 00:13:00.709
one in in the last year, I mean, now, just
like my credit cards linked up with my phone

00:13:00.709 --> 00:13:06.310
and I can swipe it, I can link Bitcoin with
my phone with instant dollar conversions and

00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:10.740
swipe at the same. So it's definitely changed.
A lot of progress has been made. And I would

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:15.649
say, you know, if you're in a place like Venezuela
or you're in a place where everything is just

00:13:15.649 --> 00:13:20.850
crumbling around you, then Bitcoin might serve
some of these purposes better than for me

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:24.290
sitting here in New Hampshire. Right. You've
got to park wealth someplace. You got to move

00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:29.490
it out of the country quickly. So it certainly
has a purpose. Now, I'll add one other caveat.

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:35.779
That may be my biggest concern, which is at
present, it's Bitcoin is a really handy medium

00:13:35.779 --> 00:13:41.629
of exchange for people doing really nefarious
things. So we just had a huge hack of a hospital

00:13:41.629 --> 00:13:46.639
system in Vermont, one of these ransomware
things where it's, you know, pay us X millions

00:13:46.639 --> 00:13:50.240
of dollars or we'll destroy all your systems.
And of course, the payment they want is in

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:57.170
Bitcoin, which is terrific for them. You know,
I do have some concerns from the Bitcoin standpoint

00:13:57.170 --> 00:14:02.949
about if this becomes the medium of exchange
for people doing bad things, then there might

00:14:02.949 --> 00:14:08.300
be some regulatory crackdown on it, in which
case it becomes less attractive as a currency

00:14:08.300 --> 00:14:12.559
for everybody else.
Yeah, that's interesting. I mean, I, I for

00:14:12.559 --> 00:14:19.999
me, the biggest concerns remain in many cases,
transaction fees and the unpredictable value

00:14:19.999 --> 00:14:25.049
that it's used for crime stuff. I mean, when
Silk Road was shut down, which was believed

00:14:25.049 --> 00:14:30.259
to be a huge piece of the Bitcoin market,
the Bitcoin market barely was affected. A

00:14:30.259 --> 00:14:34.540
number of transactions or volume. It seemed
to be a much smaller piece of it. And also,

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:39.149
by the way, I mean, most crimes are being
committed involving dollars. Most racketeering

00:14:39.149 --> 00:14:44.319
is happening. And also that one is not super
strong for me. But the logistics of of it

00:14:44.319 --> 00:14:47.699
and the volatility seem to be my biggest concerns.
I don't know.

00:14:47.699 --> 00:14:52.560
Yeah. And, you know, I think bubbles are interesting.
You never know for sure if you're in a bubble

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:58.569
until it's over. So in twenty five, I thought
the property prices were frothy. Yeah. But

00:14:58.569 --> 00:15:02.619
I didn't short the property market. It would
have been kind of hard to do. I also thought

00:15:02.619 --> 00:15:07.790
that Amazon was wildly overpriced. I was right
on one of those things and terribly wrong

00:15:07.790 --> 00:15:14.519
on the other. So I think time will tell. But
I love the discussion because I think Bitcoin

00:15:14.519 --> 00:15:19.089
forces us to think about what money is. What
are the pieces of paper issued by the Argentine

00:15:19.089 --> 00:15:23.299
government different than the pieces of paper
issued by the American company? Because it's

00:15:23.299 --> 00:15:28.779
all just paper.
Thinking about the next five years, 10 years,

00:15:28.779 --> 00:15:35.259
what types of systemic changes do you anticipate
to economic and financial systems that are

00:15:35.259 --> 00:15:41.920
a result of either technological change or
cultural change, like the big picture things?

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:47.759
I think the biggest question is what the next
phase of capitalism looks like. We're not

00:15:47.759 --> 00:15:51.829
going to go socialist. You know, I'm old enough
to have lived through the Soviet Union. I

00:15:51.829 --> 00:15:56.569
traveled there. I've been to Cuba. That's
not the future. On the other hand, I think

00:15:56.569 --> 00:16:00.860
I teach a lot of young people who look around
and say, well, something's not quite right.

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:04.230
I mean, why is the richest country in the
history of civilization? Why are people living

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:10.910
on the streets? Clearly what the populace
of both the left and the right, both Trump

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:16.259
and Bernie, who when you pass it, are saying
some of the same things, different policy,

00:16:16.259 --> 00:16:22.860
but same similar rhetoric. Yeah, right. So
exactly. Not the diagnosis is broadly similar.

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:28.999
They are reflecting the fact that despite
great wealth in over my lifetime, enormous

00:16:28.999 --> 00:16:36.170
growth in the economy and incomes, people
feel very insecure and they feel not fully

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:41.139
happy with the level of disruption. And of
course, we're looking at AEI and other things

00:16:41.139 --> 00:16:46.379
coming down the pike. So I think the question
for people who are center, right and center

00:16:46.379 --> 00:16:55.009
left, so not the socialist, not the nationalist
populist is how do we tighten the safety net

00:16:55.009 --> 00:17:01.029
so that the folks who we know are being disrupted
or will be disrupted can kind of bounce back

00:17:01.029 --> 00:17:07.100
into this vibrant, growing economy without
just hitting the ground. And interestingly,

00:17:07.100 --> 00:17:11.079
I think center left is always talked about
this. When you look at the Scandinavian countries,

